00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
military leaders in guinea are seeking  to cement their grip on power after  overthrowing the government of president  alfa conde on sunday the junta says it  will set up a transitional government  but gave no timeline the international  community has widely condemned the coup  and demanded the release of the detained  president but inside the west african  country the takeover is enjoying ample  support  a rapturous welcome for guinea's  military commanders one day after they  ceased power in a coup  some citizens hail their actions as a  move against a president who was veering  into autocracy  ministers from the ousted government  were summoned to a meeting to hear their  fate  the coup leader mamadi damboya promised  to establish a transition government but  set no timeline and made no mention of  elections  a global consultation will be opened in  order to explain the major points of the  transition  afterwards a national unity government  will be put in place to drive the  transition  dumboya is a former french legionnaire  officer who had been appointed by the  president to head an elite unit  he said he was motivated by guinea's  poverty and endemic corruption  some opposition politicians welcomed the  intervention  my message to the young generation when  you are lucky enough to be in power you  should not change the constitution  africa is awoken and the african youth  today see what is happening in other  countries we are not going to stand by  idly  words aimed at the deposed president  alfa conde seen here in a video released  by the military from an unknown location  conde is a former opposition leader and  human rights professor elected in 2010  promising to begin his nelson mandela  but after two terms in office conde  changed the constitution to allow  himself to be elected again setting off  opposition protests in which dozens were  killed  recently he introduced austerity  measures including cuts to military  spending which some observers say might  have pushed the coup leaders into action  soldiers remained on the streets on  monday searching vehicles for weapons to  prevent any counter insurgency  guinea has previously spent years under  military rule  now it has entered another period of  suspended democracy
 

